return [
    1 => [
        'title' => 'Psychotic Domain Foundations: The 5 Key Symptom Domains',
        'slug' => 'psychotic-domain-foundations-5-domains',
        'description' => 'Delusions, hallucinations, disorganized thinking/speech, grossly disorganized/abnormal motor behavior (including catatonia), and negative symptoms.',
        'overview' => "The Schizophrenia Spectrum and Other Psychotic Disorders are defined by abnormalities in one or more of five distinct symptom domains: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized thinking (speech), grossly disorganized or abnormal motor behavior (including catatonia), and negative symptoms. This categorical framework serves as the structural foundation of the entire psychotic disorders chapter in DSM-5, guiding clinical formulation and differential diagnosis across all diagnostic entities.\n\nDelusions are fixed, false beliefs that are firmly held despite clear, indisputable contradictory evidence. They are distinguished from culturally sanctioned spiritual or religious beliefs by their idiosyncratic nature and the rigidity with which they are maintained. Hallucinations are perception-like experiences that occur without an external stimulus; they are vivid, clear, have the full force and impact of normal perceptions, and are not under voluntary control, occurring predominantly in the auditory modality in schizophrenia spectrum conditions.\n\nDisorganized thinking is typically inferred from an individual's spoken speech, manifesting as loose associations, derailment, tangentiality, or incoherence ('word salad'). Abnormal motor behavior spans a spectrum from childlike silliness to unpredictable agitation, and includes catatonic behaviors characterized by marked decrease in reactivity to the environment. Negative symptoms—particularly diminished emotional expression and avolition—account for a substantial degree of the chronic morbidity and functional disability associated with schizophrenia.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "To satisfy the foundational definition of psychotic pathology under DSM-5, symptoms must be evaluated across the five core domains:\n\n1. Delusions: Evaluated as bizarre (clearly implausible, not understandable to same-culture peers, and not derived from ordinary life experiences, such as belief that internal organs were replaced without scars) or non-bizarre (thematically plausible scenarios such as being under police surveillance).\n\n2. Hallucinations: Must occur in the context of a clear sensorium; hallucinations occurring while falling asleep (hypnagogic) or waking up (hypnopompic) are normal physiological phenomena and are excluded from diagnostic consideration.\n\n3. Disorganized Speech: Must be sufficiently severe to substantially impair effective verbal communication, reflecting underlying formal thought disorder.\n\n4. Grossly Disorganized or Catatonic Behavior: Manifesting as purposeless agitation, bizarre posturing, inappropriate attire, or catatonic stupor.\n\n5. Negative Symptoms: Explicitly evaluated for Diminished Emotional Expression (reductions in facial expression, eye contact, intonation of speech) and Avolition (decrease in motivated self-initiated purposeful activities). Additional negative symptoms include Alogia (diminished speech output), Anhedonia (decreased ability to experience pleasure), and Asociality (apparent lack of interest in social interactions).",
        'specifiers' => "Psychotic phenomena can be specified according to whether delusions are 'With bizarre content'. Course specifiers for psychotic spectrum disorders include: 'First episode, currently in acute episode', 'First episode, currently in partial remission', 'First episode, currently in full remission', 'Multiple episodes', and 'Continuous'. Clinicians also specify whether the presentation is 'With catatonia'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Normal culturally shared beliefs or subcultural practices must be excluded before categorizing an idea as delusional; clinical assessment must account for the individual's religious and cultural framework.\n\nIllusions (misinterpretations of real external sensory stimuli, such as mistaking a coat rack for an intruder in a dim room) must be distinguished from true hallucinations (perceptions occurring in the total absence of external sensory input). Hallucinations occurring exclusively during substance intoxication or withdrawal, or due to a medical condition (such as temporal lobe seizures or delirium), must be ruled out.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 22-year-old college student who began isolating from friends three months ago. Over recent weeks, the student began wearing foil under their hat, claiming that satellite frequencies are transmitting thoughts into their mind (delusion with bizarre content) and reports hearing two distinct voices commenting on their daily movements (auditory hallucinations).\n\nDuring clinical interview, the student exhibits flat facial affect and monosyllabic speech (diminished emotional expression and alogia), and speaks in disjointed sentence fragments that lose logical connection (derailment). The clinical examination documents three active positive symptom domains (delusions, auditory hallucinations, disorganized speech) alongside two prominent negative symptoms (flat affect, alogia), confirming classic psychotic spectrum pathology.",
        'summary_notes' => "Essential board examination pearls: The 5 core domains of psychosis are delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized/catatonic motor behavior, and negative symptoms. Auditory hallucinations are the most frequent modality in schizophrenia. Hypnagogic (falling asleep) and hypnopompic (waking up) hallucinations are benign physiological phenomena and do not count toward psychotic diagnoses. Negative symptoms (avolition, diminished emotional expression) are the strongest predictors of long-term poor functional prognosis.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The Five Core Domains', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Delusions, hallucinations, disorganized thinking, disorganized motor behavior/catatonia, and negative symptoms form the architecture of psychotic disorders.'],
            ['title' => 'Bizarre vs. Non-Bizarre Delusions', 'icon' => '🔮', 'definition' => 'Bizarre delusions are physically impossible and culturally incomprehensible, whereas non-bizarre delusions involve plausible scenarios.'],
            ['title' => 'Auditory Hallucination Predominance', 'icon' => '👂', 'definition' => 'Auditory hallucinations (especially voices commenting or conversing) represent the primary perceptual disturbance in schizophrenia.'],
