    2 => [
        'title' => 'Brief Psychotic Disorder',
        'slug' => 'brief-psychotic-disorder',
        'description' => 'Diagnostic duration window (1 day to less than 1 month), acute precipitating stressors, and full return to premorbid functioning.',
        'overview' => "Brief Psychotic Disorder is an acute, short-lived psychotic illness characterized by the sudden onset of at least one core positive psychotic symptom (delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, or grossly disorganized/catatonic behavior) that resolves completely within one month. The defining clinical hallmark of this condition is its temporal trajectory: symptoms persist for at least one day but strictly less than one month, followed by a complete and total return to the individual's premorbid level of functioning.\n\nThe onset of Brief Psychotic Disorder is characteristically abrupt, defined in DSM-5 as a change from a nonpsychotic state to a clearly psychotic state within a two-week window. The clinical presentation is frequently intense and emotionally volatile, marked by overwhelming confusion, affective turmoil, and rapid shifts between intense emotional states, which may lead to acute behavioral disorganization and safety concerns.\n\nWhile Brief Psychotic Disorder can occur without an identifiable trigger, it frequently arises in the wake of severe psychological trauma, catastrophic psychosocial stressors (such as bereavement, natural disaster, or physical assault), or during the peripartum period. Despite the dramatic acute presentation, the long-term prognosis is generally excellent, provided that the episode fully resolves within the one-month diagnostic ceiling.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires the presence of one (or more) of the following symptoms, with at least one of symptoms 1, 2, or 3 being present: (1) Delusions; (2) Hallucinations; (3) Disorganized speech (e.g., frequent derailment or incoherence); (4) Grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior. Note: Negative symptoms are deliberately excluded from Criterion A because negative symptoms do not define acute brief psychotic states.\n\nCriterion B mandates that the duration of an episode of the disturbance is at least 1 day but less than 1 month, with eventual full return to the premorbid level of functioning.\n\nCriterion C establishes that the disturbance is not better explained by Major Depressive Disorder or Bipolar Disorder with psychotic features, or another psychotic disorder such as schizophrenia or catatonia, and is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., an illicit drug or prescribed medication) or another medical condition.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians must record one of three course specifiers: (1) 'With marked stressor(s)' (historically termed brief reactive psychosis; assigned if symptoms occur in response to events that, singly or together, would be markedly stressful to almost anyone in similar circumstances in the individual's culture); (2) 'Without marked stressor(s)' (if symptoms do not occur in response to marked stressors); or (3) 'With postpartum onset' (if onset occurs during pregnancy or within 4 weeks postpartum).\n\nAdditionally, clinicians specify whether the presentation is 'With catatonia' if catatonic motor signs accompany the acute episode.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "The primary differential boundary is determined by duration: if psychotic symptoms persist for 1 month or longer, the diagnosis must be reclassified to Schizophreniform Disorder (if lasting between 1 and 6 months) or Schizophrenia (if total disturbance persists for 6 months or longer).\n\nSubstance/Medication-Induced Psychotic Disorder must be ruled out through toxicology screening, as acute stimulant intoxication (amphetamines, cocaine) or synthetic cannabinoid use can produce acute transient psychotic states that resolve within days. Malingering and Factitious Disorder must be considered when objective external legal or financial incentives are present.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical case involves a 28-year-old individual with no prior psychiatric history who survives a catastrophic apartment fire resulting in the loss of all personal belongings. Within 48 hours, the individual becomes acutely agitated, claiming that firefighters are secret operatives planting listening devices in their clothing (delusion) and yelling in incoherent fragments (disorganized speech).\n\nToxicology screening and neurological exams are completely unremarkable. With acute supportive hospitalization and low-dose antipsychotic therapy, the psychotic symptoms resolve entirely by day 12. The individual returns to full premorbid cognitive and occupational functioning by day 18, confirming Brief Psychotic Disorder, With marked stressor.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board examination points: Brief Psychotic Disorder requires at least one positive symptom (delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, or catatonic behavior); negative symptoms do NOT count toward Criterion A. Duration must be at least 1 day and strictly less than 1 month, with a complete return to premorbid functioning. The specifier 'With marked stressor' applies when symptoms follow catastrophic events. Symptoms lasting >= 1 month must be recoded as Schizophreniform Disorder.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Strict Duration Ceiling: < 1 Month', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Symptoms must last at least 1 day and less than 30 days, terminating in full premorbid functional recovery.'],
            ['title' => 'Positive Symptoms Only (No Negative)', 'icon' => '➕', 'definition' => 'Criterion A requires at least one positive symptom (delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech); negative symptoms do not qualify.'],
            ['title' => 'With Marked Stressor Specifier', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Assigned when the acute episode is directly precipitated by severe psychosocial trauma or life catastrophe.'],
            ['title' => 'Postpartum Onset Specifier', 'icon' => '👶', 'definition' => 'Designated when psychotic symptoms emerge during pregnancy or within the first 4 weeks postpartum.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Brief Reactive Psychosis', 'definition' => 'The historical DSM-III term for Brief Psychotic Disorder occurring in direct response to severe psychosocial trauma.'],
            ['term' => 'Premorbid Functioning', 'definition' => 'The individual\'s baseline psychological, cognitive, and social level of adaptive functioning prior to the onset of the psychiatric episode.'],
            ['term' => 'Abrupt Onset', 'definition' => 'The emergence of frank psychotic symptoms within a rapid developmental window of two weeks or less from baseline.'],
            ['term' => 'Diagnostic Recoding', 'definition' => 'The clinical necessity of updating a provisional diagnosis when symptom duration exceeds the established diagnostic boundary (e.g., crossing 30 days).']
        ]
    ],
    3 => [
        'title' => 'Schizophreniform Disorder',
        'slug' => 'schizophreniform-disorder',
        'description' => 'The intermediate duration continuum (1 month to less than 6 months), provisional qualification, and prognostic indicators.',
        'overview' => "Schizophreniform Disorder occupies an essential intermediate position along the temporal continuum of psychotic disorders. Clinically, its symptom presentation is identical to that of Schizophrenia; however, its total diagnostic duration—including prodromal, active, and residual phases—is at least one month but strictly less than six months. It serves as a vital diagnostic classification that captures evolving psychotic illness without prematurely assigning the chronic, potentially stigmatizing diagnosis of Schizophrenia.\n\nA fundamental distinction between Schizophreniform Disorder and Schizophrenia is that Schizophreniform Disorder does not require a documented decline in social, occupational, or academic functioning. While functional impairment frequently occurs during acute episodes, its presence is not mandatory for establishing the diagnosis, recognizing that high-functioning individuals may experience acute psychotic illness without pervasive social breakdown.\n\nWhenever a patient is assessed before the six-month mark and full recovery has not yet occurred, the diagnosis must be designated as 'Schizophreniform Disorder (Provisional)'. If symptoms subsequently resolve prior to six months, the provisional qualifier is removed; if symptoms persist beyond six months, the diagnosis must be updated to Schizophrenia.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires that two (or more) of the following symptoms be present for a significant portion of time during a 1-month period (or less if successfully treated), with at least one of these being symptoms 1, 2, or 3: (1) Delusions; (2) Hallucinations; (3) Disorganized speech (e.g., frequent derailment or incoherence); (4) Grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior; (5) Negative symptoms (i.e., diminished emotional expression or avolition).\n\nCriterion B establishes the critical duration requirement: an episode of the disorder lasts at least 1 month but less than 6 months. When the diagnosis must be made without waiting for recovery, it should be qualified as 'provisional'.\n\nCriterion C mandates that Schizoaffective Disorder and Depressive or Bipolar Disorder with psychotic features have been ruled out because either: (1) no major depressive or manic episodes have occurred concurrently with the active-phase symptoms, or (2) if mood episodes have occurred during active-phase symptoms, they have been present for a minority of the total duration of the active and residual periods of the illness.\n\nCriterion D dictates that the disturbance is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance or another medical condition.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians specify whether the disorder is 'With good prognostic features' or 'Without good prognostic features'. A designation of 'With good prognostic features' requires the presence of at least two of the following four clinical characteristics: (1) onset of prominent psychotic symptoms within 4 weeks of the first noticeable change in usual behavior or functioning; (2) confusion or perplexity at the height of the psychotic episode; (3) good premorbid social and occupational functioning; and (4) absence of blunted or flat affect.\n\nThe absence of two or more of these features warrants the specifier 'Without good prognostic features', which is associated with a substantially higher risk of progression to chronic Schizophrenia.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "The primary differential distinction is temporal: Brief Psychotic Disorder resolves in less than 1 month, while Schizophrenia requires persistent signs of disturbance for at least 6 months.\n\nSchizoaffective Disorder must be excluded; in Schizoaffective Disorder, a major mood episode is present for the majority of the illness's active and residual duration, whereas in Schizophreniform Disorder, mood symptoms are either absent or present for only a minor fraction of the total course. Substance-induced psychosis must be ruled out via clinical history and toxicology screens.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 20-year-old university student who developed auditory hallucinations and persecutory delusions two months ago, believing that university professors were altering their grades through cyberwarfare. The student's speech is tangential, and they exhibit psychomotor agitation.\n\nToxicology and medical workups are completely negative. Because active Criterion A psychotic symptoms have persisted for exactly two months (exceeding the 1-month ceiling of Brief Psychotic Disorder but falling well below the 6-month threshold of Schizophrenia), the accurate diagnosis is Schizophreniform Disorder (Provisional).",
        'summary_notes' => "Key licensure board pearls: Schizophreniform Disorder shares identical Criterion A symptom requirements with Schizophrenia, but differs by duration (at least 1 month and less than 6 months). Unlike Schizophrenia, Schizophreniform Disorder does NOT require social or occupational impairment. The specifier 'With good prognostic features' requires at least 2 of 4 indicators (acute onset within 4 weeks, confusion/perplexity, good premorbid functioning, absence of flat affect). If symptoms pass 6 months, the diagnosis must be recoded to Schizophrenia.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 1 to 6 Month Window', 'icon' => '📅', 'definition' => 'Symptoms persist for at least 30 days but resolve before 180 days, representing an intermediate psychotic continuum.'],
            ['title' => 'No Impairment Requirement', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'Unlike Schizophrenia, formal functional or occupational decline is not required to establish the diagnosis.'],
            ['title' => 'Provisional Qualifier', 'icon' => '🏷️', 'definition' => 'Assigned when diagnosing a patient who has been ill for 1-5 months and has not yet achieved full recovery.'],
            ['title' => 'Good Prognostic Features Specifier', 'icon' => '🌟', 'definition' => 'Requires 2 of 4 markers: acute onset within 4 weeks, confusion at peak, good premorbid function, and absence of flat affect.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Schizophreniform', 'definition' => 'Formally resembling schizophrenia in clinical symptomatology while differing strictly in duration and lack of required functional decline.'],
            ['term' => 'Provisional Diagnosis', 'definition' => 'A temporary diagnostic designation used when clinical criteria are currently met but the ultimate duration threshold remains pending future observation.'],
            ['term' => 'Perplexity', 'definition' => 'A state of cognitive disorientation and bewilderment often observed during acute, sudden-onset psychotic episodes.'],
            ['term' => 'Symptom Trajectory', 'definition' => 'The longitudinal progression of psychiatric symptoms over time, critical for defining diagnostic boundaries across the psychotic spectrum.']
        ]
    ],
    4 => [
        'title' => 'Schizophrenia: Active Phase & The 6-Month Criterion',
        'slug' => 'schizophrenia-active-phase-6-month-criterion',
        'description' => 'The 6-month chronicity threshold, Criterion A active phase requirements, functional deterioration, and elimination of DSM-IV classic subtypes.',
        'overview' => "Schizophrenia is a severe, chronic neuropsychiatric disorder characterized by profound disruptions in thinking, perception, emotional responsiveness, and interpersonal behavior. In DSM-5, the diagnostic criteria require continuous signs of the disturbance to persist for at least six months, including at least one full month of active-phase symptoms (or less if successfully treated), accompanied by marked functional deterioration in self-care, interpersonal relations, or occupational achievement.\n\nA major structural evolution in DSM-5 was the complete elimination of the classical DSM-IV clinical subtypes (Paranoid, Disorganized, Catatonic, Undifferentiated, and Residual). Decades of empirical psychiatric research demonstrated that these historical subtypes exhibited poor diagnostic stability over time, low inter-rater reliability, and minimal predictive validity regarding treatment response or genetic etiology. Instead, DSM-5 adopted a dimensional approach that rates the severity of core symptom dimensions.\n\nFurthermore, DSM-5 tightened Criterion A requirements. Under DSM-IV, a patient could receive a diagnosis of schizophrenia based on a single symptom if they exhibited 'Schneiderian' bizarre delusions or running commentary auditory hallucinations. DSM-5 eliminated this exception, mandating that all patients must exhibit at least two Criterion A symptoms, with at least one symptom being delusions, hallucinations, or disorganized speech.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A (Active-Phase Symptoms) requires two (or more) of the following, each present for a significant portion of time during a 1-month period (or less if successfully treated), with at least one being 1, 2, or 3: (1) Delusions; (2) Hallucinations; (3) Disorganized speech; (4) Grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior; (5) Negative symptoms (diminished emotional expression or avolition).\n\nCriterion B (Functional Deterioration) requires that for a significant portion of time since onset, level of functioning in one or more major areas (work, interpersonal relations, or self-care) is markedly below the level achieved prior to onset (or failure to achieve expected interpersonal/academic level in children/adolescents).\n\nCriterion C (Duration) mandates continuous signs of the disturbance for at least 6 months. This 6-month period must include at least 1 month of symptoms that meet Criterion A (active-phase symptoms) and may include periods of prodromal or residual symptoms. During prodromal or residual periods, signs of the disturbance may be manifested by only negative symptoms or by two or more Criterion A symptoms in an attenuated form (e.g., odd beliefs, unusual perceptual experiences).\n\nCriterion D and E require ruling out Schizoaffective Disorder, mood disorders with psychotic features, substance effects, and medical conditions.",
        'specifiers' => "Course specifiers are coded after a 1-year duration: 'First episode, currently in acute episode', 'First episode, currently in partial remission', 'First episode, currently in full remission', 'Multiple episodes, currently in acute episode', 'Multiple episodes, currently in partial remission', 'Multiple episodes, currently in full remission', and 'Continuous'.\n\nClinicians also specify whether the presentation is 'With catatonia'. Severity of core symptom domains can be rated dimensionally on the Clinician-Rated Dimensions of Psychosis Symptom Severity scale.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Schizoaffective Disorder is the most critical differential; in Schizoaffective Disorder, a major depressive or manic episode is concurrent with active-phase symptoms and present for the majority of the total illness duration, AND delusions or hallucinations must occur for at least 2 weeks in the absence of a major mood episode.\n\nMajor Depressive Disorder or Bipolar Disorder with Psychotic Features: Psychotic symptoms occur exclusively during mood episodes. Autism Spectrum Disorder: If a patient has a history of ASD, the additional diagnosis of Schizophrenia is made ONLY if prominent delusions or hallucinations are present for at least 1 month.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 24-year-old individual brought for evaluation by family members. Seven months ago, the patient became socially withdrawn, quit their job, and neglected personal hygiene (prodromal phase and Criterion B functional decline). Over the past three months, the patient has expressed fixed beliefs that neighbors are broadcasting ionizing radiation into their bedroom (persecutory delusions), hears voices conversing through electrical outlets (auditory hallucinations), and speaks in disjointed fragments.\n\nMedical and toxicological screens are entirely normal. Because continuous signs of the disturbance have persisted for seven months, including three months of active Criterion A symptoms alongside severe functional deterioration, the clinical formulation confirms a diagnosis of Schizophrenia.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Schizophrenia requires continuous signs of disturbance for >= 6 months, including >= 1 month of active Criterion A symptoms. At least one of the Criterion A symptoms MUST be delusions, hallucinations, or disorganized speech. DSM-5 eliminated the classic subtypes (Paranoid, Disorganized, Catatonic, etc.). DSM-5 eliminated the Schneiderian single-symptom exception; two symptoms are always required. In patients with pre-existing Autism Spectrum Disorder, Schizophrenia can only be diagnosed if prominent delusions or hallucinations are present for >= 1 month.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 6-Month Chronicity Rule', 'icon' => '🗓️', 'definition' => 'Requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least 6 months, incorporating active, prodromal, and residual phases.'],
            ['title' => 'Mandatory Core Positive Symptoms', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'At least one Criterion A symptom must be delusions, hallucinations, or disorganized speech.'],
            ['title' => 'Elimination of Classic Subtypes', 'icon' => '🗑️', 'definition' => 'Paranoid, disorganized, catatonic, undifferentiated, and residual subtypes were removed due to low stability and validity.'],
            ['title' => 'Functional Impairment Requirement', 'icon' => '📉', 'definition' => 'Requires marked deterioration in occupational, interpersonal, or self-care functioning compared to premorbid baseline.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Prodromal Phase', 'definition' => 'The early period preceding active psychosis, characterized by insidious deterioration in functioning, social withdrawal, odd beliefs, and attenuated perceptual distortions.'],
            ['term' => 'Residual Phase', 'definition' => 'The period following an active psychotic episode where overt positive symptoms have abated, but negative symptoms, eccentric thinking, and functional impairment persist.'],
            ['term' => 'Schneiderian First-Rank Symptoms', 'definition' => 'Historical psychotic symptoms (e.g., thought broadcasting, voices conversing) once given single-symptom diagnostic priority in DSM-IV, now requiring standard multi-symptom confirmation in DSM-5.'],
            ['term' => 'Thought Broadcasting', 'definition' => 'The delusional belief that one\'s silent thoughts are radiating outward and being perceived or overheard by individuals in the external environment.']
        ]
    ],
    5 => [
        'title' => 'Schizoaffective Disorder',
        'slug' => 'schizoaffective-disorder',
        'description' => 'The 2-week psychotic window rule, concurrent mood episodes, longitudinal mood-to-psychosis ratios, and subtype categorization.',
        'overview' => "Schizoaffective Disorder represents a complex diagnostic entity that bridges the clinical spectrum between Schizophrenia and severe Mood Disorders. Diagnostically, it is characterized by an uninterrupted period of illness during which an individual meets full Criterion A criteria for Schizophrenia concurrently with a Major Depressive Episode or a Manic Episode.\n\nThe critical diagnostic cornerstone that separates Schizoaffective Disorder from a Mood Disorder with Psychotic Features is the 'two-week rule': delusions or hallucinations must be present for at least two consecutive weeks in the complete absence of a major mood episode at some point during the lifetime course of the illness. This criterion establishes that the psychotic pathology is independent and capable of sustaining itself outside the context of affective dysregulation.\n\nIn DSM-5, the conceptual definition was significantly refined to mandate a longitudinal perspective. DSM-5 requires that symptoms meeting criteria for a major mood episode must be present for the majority of the total duration of the active and residual periods of the illness. This longitudinal requirement prevents patients with chronic schizophrenia who experience brief, transient depressive episodes from being misdiagnosed with schizoaffective disorder.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires an uninterrupted period of illness during which there is a major mood episode (Major Depressive or Manic) concurrent with Criterion A of schizophrenia. (Note: The major depressive episode must include Criterion A1: depressed mood; anhedonia alone is not sufficient).\n\nCriterion B mandates delusions or hallucinations for 2 or more weeks in the absence of a major mood episode (depressive or manic) during the lifetime duration of the illness.\n\nCriterion C establishes the longitudinal criterion: symptoms that meet criteria for a major mood episode are present for the majority of the total duration of the active and residual portions of the illness.\n\nCriterion D dictates that the disturbance is not attributable to the effects of a substance or another medical condition.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians specify the diagnostic subtype based on mood presentation: (1) 'Bipolar type' (assigned if a manic episode is part of the presentation; major depressive episodes may also occur); or (2) 'Depressive type' (assigned if only major depressive episodes are part of the presentation, with no history of mania).\n\nAdditionally, clinicians specify whether the condition is 'With catatonia' and assign standard longitudinal course specifiers (acute episode, partial remission, full remission).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Schizoaffective Disorder vs. Mood Disorder with Psychotic Features: In Mood Disorder with Psychotic Features, psychotic symptoms occur exclusively during active mood episodes. If delusions or hallucinations persist for >= 2 weeks after the mood episode resolves, the diagnosis MUST be Schizoaffective Disorder or Schizophrenia.\n\nSchizoaffective Disorder vs. Schizophrenia: In Schizophrenia, mood episodes are present for only a minority of the total active and residual duration of the illness; in Schizoaffective Disorder, mood episodes occupy the majority of the total duration.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 32-year-old patient evaluated over a three-year clinical timeline. During the initial two years, the patient experienced chronic persecutory delusions and auditory hallucinations concurrent with severe major depressive episodes meeting full criteria for 18 of the 24 months.\n\nHowever, during an eight-week window between depressive episodes when mood was completely euthymic, the patient continued to experience persistent auditory hallucinations and fixed paranoid delusions without affective disturbance. Because mood symptoms were present for the majority of the total illness duration (18 of 24 months) AND psychotic symptoms persisted for >2 weeks in the absence of mood disturbance, the diagnosis is confirmed as Schizoaffective Disorder, Depressive type.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board examination points: The defining rule for Schizoaffective Disorder is delusions or hallucinations for >= 2 weeks in the ABSENCE of a major mood episode. Mood symptoms must be present for the MAJORITY of the total illness duration. The Bipolar type applies if mania has ever occurred; Depressive type applies if only major depression has occurred. For major depressive episodes in Criterion A, depressed mood (Criterion A1) is mandatory—anhedonia alone does not suffice.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 2-Week Psychotic Window', 'icon' => '🪟', 'definition' => 'Delusions or hallucinations must persist for at least 2 weeks without major mood symptoms to prove independent psychotic pathology.'],
            ['title' => 'Majority Mood Duration Rule', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Symptoms of a major mood episode must occupy the majority (>50%) of the total active and residual course of the illness.'],
            ['title' => 'Bipolar vs. Depressive Subtypes', 'icon' => '🎭', 'definition' => 'Bipolar type is assigned if mania is ever documented; Depressive type is reserved for presentations involving purely depressive episodes.'],
            ['title' => 'Mandatory Depressed Mood', 'icon' => '🌧️', 'definition' => 'Major depressive episodes in schizoaffective disorder must include depressed mood; pervasive loss of interest alone does not satisfy criteria.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Schizoaffective', 'definition' => 'A disorder featuring concurrent schizophrenic psychosis and affective mood episodes, alongside independent psychotic persistence.'],
            ['term' => 'The 2-Week Rule', 'definition' => 'The clinical requirement that psychotic symptoms occur for at least 14 days in the total absence of significant mood disturbance.'],
            ['term' => 'Longitudinal Proportion', 'definition' => 'Evaluating the ratio of time an individual experiences mood symptoms relative to the overall duration of their psychotic illness.'],
            ['term' => 'Mood-Congruent Psychosis', 'definition' => 'Delusions or hallucinations whose content is entirely consistent with typical depressive (guilt, ruin) or manic (grandiosity, power) themes.']
        ]
    ],
    6 => [
        'title' => 'Delusional Disorder & Subtypes',
        'slug' => 'delusional-disorder-subtypes',
        'description' => 'The 1-month duration threshold, absence of prominent hallucinations, preserved functional integrity, and 7 core clinical subtypes.',
        'overview' => "Delusional Disorder is characterized by the presence of one or more delusions that persist for at least one month in the absence of other prominent psychotic symptoms. A defining characteristic of Delusional Disorder is that, apart from the direct impact of the delusion or its practical ramifications, psychosocial functioning is not markedly impaired and behavior is not obviously bizarre or odd.\n\nIndividuals with Delusional Disorder typically retain intact cognitive faculties, logical reasoning in non-delusional spheres, and normal communicative abilities. For instance, an individual who falsely believes their spouse is unfaithful may maintain excellent occupational performance, coherent conversation, and appropriate self-care, exhibiting distress or confrontation only when topics related to their marital fidelity arise.\n\nIn DSM-5, delusions in Delusional Disorder can now be specified as 'bizarre', departing from DSM-IV which historically restricted the diagnosis to non-bizarre delusions. If hallucinations are present, they are not prominent and are directly related to the delusional theme (e.g., sensation of insects crawling on the skin in an individual with delusions of parasitic infestation).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires the presence of one (or more) delusions with a duration of 1 month or longer.\n\nCriterion B mandates that Criterion A for schizophrenia has NEVER been met. (Note: Hallucinations, if present, are not prominent and are related to the delusional theme).\n\nCriterion C establishes that apart from the impact of the delusion(s) or its ramifications, functioning is not markedly impaired, and behavior is not obviously bizarre or odd.\n\nCriterion D dictates that if manic or major depressive episodes have occurred, these have been brief relative to the duration of the delusional periods.\n\nCriterion E requires that the disturbance is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance or another medical condition and is not better explained by another mental disorder (such as Body Dysmorphic Disorder or Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder).",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians assign one of seven specific subtypes based on the predominant delusional theme: (1) 'Erotomanic type' (belief that another person, usually of higher status, is in love with the individual); (2) 'Grandiose type' (conviction of having great unrecognized talent, insight, or special relationship with a deity or prominent person); (3) 'Jealous type' (belief that one's spouse or lover is unfaithful); (4) 'Persecutory type' (conviction of being conspired against, spied on, cheated, poisoned, or maligned; the most common subtype); (5) 'Somatic type' (delusions involving bodily functions or sensations, such as foul odors or infestation); (6) 'Mixed type' (delusions characteristic of more than one type, with no single theme predominating); and (7) 'Unspecified type'.\n\nClinicians also specify if the presentation is 'With bizarre content' (if the delusion is clearly implausible and incomprehensible).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Delusional Disorder vs. Schizophrenia: Schizophrenia requires at least two Criterion A symptoms (e.g., prominent hallucinations, disorganized speech, negative symptoms) and marked functional deterioration; Delusional Disorder features circumscribed delusions with preserved general functioning.\n\nDelusional Disorder, Somatic type vs. Illness Anxiety Disorder: In Illness Anxiety Disorder, the individual fears having a disease but acknowledges the possibility that the fear is unfounded; in Delusional Disorder, the conviction is held with absolute delusional certainty.\n\nDelusional Disorder, Jealous type vs. Normal Jealousy: Delusional jealousy is founded on incorrect inferences unsupported by evidence, resistant to all contradictory proof, and leads to intense intrusive interrogations.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical case involves a 45-year-old executive who performs exceptionally at work and maintains normal friendships. For the past six months, the individual has held an unshakeable conviction that a famous television anchor sends secret love codes through subtle tie color adjustments during nightly broadcasts (erotomanic delusion).\n\nThe individual has sent hundreds of letters to the studio declaring mutual love. Clinical examination reveals no auditory hallucinations, no disorganized speech, and completely normal self-care and professional functioning. The clinical formulation confirms Delusional Disorder, Erotomanic type.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board examination points: Delusional Disorder requires at least 1 delusion lasting >= 1 month. Crucially, general psychosocial functioning is NOT markedly impaired outside the sphere of the delusion. The 5 primary subtypes are Erotomanic, Grandiose, Jealous, Persecutory (most common), and Somatic. Hallucinations, if present at all, must be minor and directly thematic to the delusion. DSM-5 allows the 'With bizarre content' specifier.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Circumscribed Delusional Focus', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'Delusions persist without contaminating general cognitive faculties, social decorum, or occupational competencies.'],
            ['title' => 'Preserved Everyday Functioning', 'icon' => '💼', 'definition' => 'Outside the direct scope of the delusional theme, the individual\'s behavior is not markedly impaired or bizarre.'],
            ['title' => 'The 1-Month Duration Floor', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Fixed delusional beliefs must be documented for at least 30 consecutive days.'],
            ['title' => 'Erotomanic & Jealous Subtypes', 'icon' => '💌', 'definition' => 'Erotomanic involves belief a prominent person is in love with them; Jealous involves unshakeable conviction of partner infidelity.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Erotomania (Clérambault Syndrome)', 'definition' => 'A delusional belief that another person, typically of higher social or economic standing, is secretly in love with the patient.'],
            ['term' => 'Othello Syndrome', 'definition' => 'The clinical eponym for Delusional Disorder, Jealous type, marked by unshakeable delusions of partner infidelity without justification.'],
            ['term' => 'Delusional Parasitosis (Ekbom Syndrome)', 'definition' => 'A Somatic type delusion characterized by the unshakable conviction that one\'s body or skin is infested with insects, parasites, or worms.'],
            ['term' => 'Encapsulated Delusion', 'definition' => 'A delusion that remains isolated from the rest of the patient\'s personality and cognitive functioning without causing widespread behavioral deterioration.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Catatonia Associated with Another Mental Disorder / Specifier',
        'slug' => 'catatonia-associated-mental-disorder-specifier',
        'description' => 'The 12 defining motor signs, 3-symptom diagnostic threshold, neurobiology, and clinical management.',
        'overview' => "Catatonia is a complex neuropsychiatric syndrome characterized by marked psychomotor disturbances that may involve decreased motor activity, decreased engagement during interview or physical examination, or excessive and peculiar motor activity. Historically viewed almost exclusively as a subtype of schizophrenia, contemporary psychiatric research demonstrates that catatonia occurs far more frequently in association with severe Mood Disorders (particularly Bipolar Mania and Major Depressive Disorder), medical conditions, or as an adverse medication effect.\n\nIn DSM-5, catatonia was definitively eliminated as an independent schizophrenia subtype and established as a transdiagnostic specifier that can be applied to any neurodevelopmental, psychotic, bipolar, depressive, or other mental disorder, as well as a standalone diagnosis when secondary to an underlying medical condition.\n\nThe pathophysiology of catatonia involves acute dysregulation within GABA-A, dopamine D2, and NMDA receptor systems in the orbitofrontal cortex and basal ganglia. Catatonic states require urgent clinical recognition because malignant forms—characterized by severe autonomic instability, hyperthermia, and rigidity—carry substantial mortality risks if not treated promptly with benzodiazepines or electroconvulsive therapy (ECT).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Under DSM-5, the clinical diagnosis of Catatonia requires the presence of three (or more) of the following 12 distinct psychomotor features:\n\n1. Stupor: No psychomotor activity; not actively relating to environment.\n2. Catalepsy: Passive induction of a posture held against gravity.\n3. Waxy Flexibility: Slight, even resistance to positioning by examiner.\n4. Mutism: No, or very little, verbal response (exclude if known aphasia).\n5. Negativism: Opposition or no response to instructions or external stimuli.\n6. Posturing: Spontaneous and active maintenance of a posture against gravity.\n7. Mannerism: Odd, circumstantial caricature of normal actions.\n8. Stereotypy: Repetitive, abnormally frequent, non-goal-directed movements.\n9. Agitation: Extreme psychomotor excitement not influenced by external stimuli.\n10. Grimacing: Maintenance of odd, distorted facial expressions.\n11. Echolalia: Mimicking another's speech.\n12. Echopraxia: Mimicking another's movements.",
        'specifiers' => "Catatonia is specified according to its primary diagnostic context: 'Catatonia Associated with Another Mental Disorder' (applied as a specifier to Schizophrenia, Major Depressive Disorder, Bipolar Disorder, etc.); 'Catatonic Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition' (identifying organic causes such as autoimmune encephalitis, lupus, or metabolic derangements); or 'Unspecified Catatonia' (used in acute emergency settings when the underlying etiology is not yet determined).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS): Shares severe rigidity, hyperthermia, and autonomic instability following antipsychotic administration; catatonia frequently responds rapidly to high-dose lorazepam, whereas NMS requires immediate cessation of dopamine antagonists and intensive medical stabilization.\n\nExtrapyramidal Parkinsonian rigidity: Parkinsonism features 'cogwheel' rigidity and resting tremor, whereas catatonia features waxy flexibility, catalepsy, negativism, and mutism. Selective Mutism involves anxiety-mediated refusal to speak in specific social environments, without the motor catalepsy, waxy flexibility, or posturing seen in catatonia.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 35-year-old patient with severe bipolar mania admitted to the inpatient psychiatry unit. Over 24 hours, the patient ceases speaking entirely (mutism) and remains motionless in bed without initiating fluid or food intake (stupor).\n\nWhen the examiner raises the patient's arm into the air, the patient maintains the uncomfortable elevated position against gravity for over twenty minutes (catalepsy and posturing), and offers slight, smooth plastic resistance to limb movement (waxy flexibility). Meeting four criteria (mutism, stupor, catalepsy, waxy flexibility), the diagnosis is documented as Bipolar I Disorder, Current Episode Manic, With Catatonia.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board examination points: Catatonia requires at least 3 of 12 defining psychomotor features. DSM-5 eliminated catatonia as a standalone schizophrenia subtype, establishing it as a transdiagnostic specifier across mood and psychotic disorders. Catatonia is most frequently seen in Mood Disorders (Bipolar mania and depression), not Schizophrenia. The Lorazepam (Ativan) challenge test is both diagnostic and therapeutic; electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) is the definitive treatment for refractory or malignant catatonia.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => '3 of 12 Diagnostic Threshold', 'icon' => '🔢', 'definition' => 'Requires the presence of at least three classic motor signs from the 12-item DSM-5 catatonia menu.'],
            ['title' => 'Transdiagnostic Specifier Status', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Can be applied to Schizophrenia, Bipolar Disorder, Major Depressive Disorder, or coded as secondary to medical conditions.'],
            ['title' => 'Mood Disorder Association Predominance', 'icon' => '🎭', 'definition' => 'Occurs more frequently in severe affective episodes (bipolar mania and depression) than in schizophrenia.'],
            ['title' => 'Lorazepam & ECT Responsiveness', 'icon' => '💉', 'definition' => 'GABA-A agonism via intravenous lorazepam provides rapid diagnostic confirmation and clinical reversal.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Waxy Flexibility (Flexibilitas Cerea)', 'definition' => 'A physical finding where the patient\'s limbs offer smooth, plastic resistance when moved by the examiner, akin to bending a warm candle.'],
            ['term' => 'Catalepsy', 'definition' => 'Passive induction and unprompted maintenance of an awkward posture against gravity for prolonged periods.'],
            ['term' => 'Negativism', 'definition' => 'Active resistance to or motiveless opposition to all instructions, attempts to move the patient, or environmental stimuli.'],
            ['term' => 'Malignant Catatonia', 'definition' => 'A life-threatening medical emergency featuring acute catatonic motor signs alongside severe autonomic instability, hyperthermia, and altered consciousness.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Substance/Medication-Induced Psychotic Disorder & Medical Conditions',
        'slug' => 'substance-induced-psychotic-disorder-medical-conditions',
        'description' => 'Temporal etiology rules, toxicological mimicry, physiological mechanisms, and organic medical rule-outs.',
        'overview' => "A fundamental clinical obligation when evaluating psychotic presentations is the rigorous exclusion of substance/medication physiological effects and general medical etiologies. Psychotic symptoms secondary to organic or pharmacological triggers can present with clinical features identical to primary idiopathic schizophrenia spectrum disorders, yet require radically different, time-sensitive medical interventions.\n\nSubstance/Medication-Induced Psychotic Disorder is diagnosed when delusions and/or hallucinations develop during or soon after substance intoxication, substance withdrawal, or after exposure to a medication capable of producing psychotic phenomena. Common culprits include stimulants (cocaine, amphetamines, methamphetamine), phencyclidine (PCP), cannabis, synthetic cannabinoids, alcohol withdrawal delirium, corticosteroids, and anticholinergic medications.\n\nConversely, Psychotic Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition is established when delusions or hallucinations are the direct pathophysiological consequence of a somatic, neurological, or endocrine illness. Common medical causes include anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis, systemic lupus erythematosus (lupus cerebritis), temporal lobe epilepsy, acute porphyria, Huntington's disease, Wilson's disease, and neurosyphilis.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "For Substance/Medication-Induced Psychotic Disorder: Criterion A requires prominent delusions or hallucinations. Criterion B requires evidence from history, physical exam, or laboratory findings that symptoms developed during or soon after intoxication, withdrawal, or medication exposure, and the substance is capable of producing the symptoms. Criterion C mandates that the disturbance is not better explained by an independent psychotic disorder (e.g., psychotic symptoms preceded substance use, or persist for a substantial period—such as more than 1 month—after cessation of acute withdrawal or intoxication). Criterion D requires that symptoms do not occur exclusively during delirium.\n\nFor Psychotic Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition: Criterion A requires prominent delusions or hallucinations. Criterion B requires evidence that the disturbance is the direct pathophysiological consequence of another medical condition. Criterion C mandates that the disturbance is not better explained by another mental disorder. Criterion D requires that symptoms do not occur exclusively during delirium.",
        'specifiers' => "For Substance/Medication-Induced Psychotic Disorder, clinicians record the specific offending substance and specify whether onset occurred 'With onset during intoxication' or 'With onset during withdrawal'.\n\nFor Psychotic Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition, clinicians record the medical condition (e.g., due to anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis) and specify the predominant presentation: 'With delusions' or 'With hallucinations'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Delirium: If psychotic symptoms occur in the context of fluctuating attention, disorientation, and clouded consciousness, the diagnosis is Delirium, NOT a psychotic disorder.\n\nPrimary Schizophrenia Spectrum Disorder: In primary schizophrenia, psychotic symptoms typically have an insidious onset, often persist indefinitely without substance exposure, and frequently feature prominent negative symptoms and early developmental social cognitive deficits.\n\nSubstance Intoxication or Withdrawal without Psychosis: Transient perceptual distortions or illusions where reality testing remains intact (e.g., recognizing that an image is an illusion) do not warrant a diagnosis of psychotic disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 19-year-old college student with no prior psychiatric history admitted to the emergency department with acute persecutory delusions, auditory hallucinations, insomnia, and autonomic instability (tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis, dilated pupils) that began 12 hours ago.\n\nUrine toxicology screen is strongly positive for high-concentration methamphetamine. Over 48 hours in a quiet medical stabilization unit with hydration and supportive care, the delusions and hallucinations resolve completely in parallel with the clearance of the stimulant. The accurate diagnosis is Substance/Medication-Induced Psychotic Disorder, with onset during intoxication, due to methamphetamine.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board examination points: If psychotic symptoms occur exclusively during the course of a Delirium (fluctuating alertness, disorientation), Delirium is diagnosed—never a psychotic disorder. Symptoms persisting >1 month after complete cessation of substance intoxication/withdrawal suggest an independent primary psychotic disorder. Always order toxicology screens and evaluate for organic causes (e.g., anti-NMDA encephalitis, Wilson disease, lupus, temporal lobe epilepsy) before diagnosing first-episode schizophrenia in young adults.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Temporal Exposure Link', 'icon' => '⏳', 'definition' => 'Symptoms must emerge during or immediately following substance intoxication, withdrawal, or medication administration.'],
            ['title' => 'Exclusion During Delirium', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'If psychotic phenomena manifest exclusively during an acute delirium, the diagnosis of delirium takes absolute precedence.'],
            ['title' => 'The 1-Month Persistence Boundary', 'icon' => '📅', 'definition' => 'Psychosis persisting longer than 4 weeks following complete detoxification strongly indicates an underlying independent primary disorder.'],
            ['title' => 'Organic Neurological Etiologies', 'icon' => '🔬', 'definition' => 'Encephalitis, autoimmune cerebritis, metabolic disorders, and epilepsy must be excluded via neuroimaging and laboratory tests.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis', 'definition' => 'An autoimmune neurological condition characterized by antibodies targeting NMDA receptors, frequently presenting with acute psychosis, catatonia, seizures, and autonomic instability in young females.'],
            ['term' => 'Toxic Psychosis', 'definition' => 'A state of delusions and hallucinations directly induced by pharmacological toxicity from high-dose stimulants, hallucinogens, or anticholinergic compounds.'],
            ['term' => 'Reality Testing', 'definition' => 'The psychological capacity to distinguish internal cognitive fantasies and perceptions from objective external reality.'],
            ['term' => 'Formication', 'definition' => 'A specific tactile hallucination involving the sensation of insects crawling on or under the skin, classically associated with cocaine and methamphetamine toxicity.']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Differential Diagnosis: Psychotic Spectrum vs. Mood Disorders vs. Medical Mimics',
        'slug' => 'differential-diagnosis-psychotic-mood-medical',
        'description' => 'Systematic differential algorithm: duration thresholds, mood-to-psychosis ratios, and medical-organic boundary distinctions.',
        'overview' => "Navigating the differential diagnosis of psychotic spectrum disorders requires a rigorous, hierarchical clinical decision-making algorithm. Because psychotic symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech) represent final common behavioral pathways for diverse biological insults, arriving at the correct diagnostic classification carries profound prognostic, legal, and pharmacological ramifications.\n\nThe initial branch point in the differential hierarchy demands the absolute exclusion of medical, neurological, and toxicological etiologies. Once organic causes and delirium are eliminated, the clinician must analyze the chronological relationship between psychotic symptoms and mood episodes. If mood symptoms are absent, the diagnosis is governed strictly by duration (Brief Psychotic Disorder <1 month; Schizophreniform Disorder 1-6 months; Schizophrenia >=6 months; Delusional Disorder >=1 month with circumscribed delusions and preserved functioning).\n\nIf mood episodes are prominent, the clinician must determine the longitudinal mood-to-psychosis ratio and whether psychotic symptoms ever persist independently. This module provides a systematic clinical framework to resolve complex diagnostic dilemmas across psychotic, affective, and personality presentations.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Differential Decision Hierarchy:\n\n1. Step 1: Medical & Toxicological Exclusion. Rule out substance intoxication/withdrawal, medication side effects, and general medical conditions (anti-NMDA encephalitis, lupus, thyroid disease, epilepsy). Verify absence of Delirium.\n\n2. Step 2: Evaluate Mood Episode Co-occurrence. If major depressive or manic episodes are present:\n   • If psychosis occurs ONLY during mood episodes -> Mood Disorder with Psychotic Features.\n   • If psychosis occurs for >= 2 weeks WITHOUT mood symptoms, AND mood episodes occupy the majority of total illness duration -> Schizoaffective Disorder.\n   • If mood episodes occupy only a minority of total illness duration -> Schizophrenia.\n\n3. Step 3: Pure Psychotic Duration Stratification (When Mood Symptoms are Absent):\n   • 1 day to < 1 month -> Brief Psychotic Disorder.\n   • 1 month to < 6 months -> Schizophreniform Disorder.\n   • >= 6 months with functional decline -> Schizophrenia.\n   • >= 1 month of circumscribed delusions without prominent hallucinations and with preserved everyday functioning -> Delusional Disorder.",
        'specifiers' => "Differential formulations must specify: (1) Primary psychiatric diagnosis; (2) Associated specifiers (e.g., 'With marked stressor', 'With good prognostic features', 'Bipolar type', 'With catatonia'); and (3) Secondary comorbid conditions or provisional indicators.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) with Absent Insight / Delusional Beliefs: In OCD, the belief is preceded by intrusive obsessional doubts and compulsive rituals, whereas primary delusions lack compulsive neutralizing rituals.\n\nBorderline Personality Disorder: Transient, stress-related paranoid ideation or dissociative symptoms in BPD are brief (lasting hours), fluctuate rapidly with interpersonal conflict, and lack sustained fixed delusional certainty.\n\nIllness Anxiety Disorder vs. Somatic Delusion: Illness anxiety features fear of having an illness with fluctuating doubt; Delusional disorder features absolute unshakeable conviction of somatic infestation or disease.",
        'clinical_example' => "A clinical vignette involves a 26-year-old patient who presents with persecutory delusions, auditory hallucinations, and social withdrawal for four months, without any history of mood episodes. The patient's general functioning has declined significantly.\n\nToxicology and medical testing are negative. Walking through the differential algorithm: Organic/toxic causes are ruled out. Mood episodes are absent. Duration is exactly four months (exceeding 30 days but under 6 months). Therefore, the correct diagnosis is Schizophreniform Disorder (Provisional), not Schizophrenia (requires >=6 months) and not Brief Psychotic Disorder (requires <1 month).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Memorize the temporal duration boundaries: Brief Psychotic (<1 mo) -> Schizophreniform (1-6 mos) -> Schizophrenia (>=6 mos). The 2-week rule (psychosis without mood) is the litmus test distinguishing Schizoaffective Disorder from Mood Disorder with Psychotic Features. Delusional Disorder requires preserved everyday functioning. Always rule out Delirium, substance intoxication, and medical conditions prior to assigning primary psychiatric diagnoses.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Three-Step Decision Algorithm', 'icon' => '🪜', 'definition' => 'Systematic clinical process: (1) Medical/toxic rule-out, (2) Mood vs. psychosis ratio, (3) Temporal duration stratification.'],
            ['title' => 'The Psychotic Timeline Scale', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Precise recognition of duration demarcations: <1 month, 1-6 months, and >=6 months across primary psychotic diagnoses.'],
            ['title' => 'The Affective Boundary Test', 'icon' => '🎭', 'definition' => 'Evaluating whether psychotic phenomena persist during euthymic periods versus emerging strictly during peak mood disturbances.'],
            ['title' => 'OCD vs. Delusion Boundary', 'icon' => '🔒', 'definition' => 'Distinguishing ego-dystonic obsessions with poor insight from primary fixed ego-syntonic delusions.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Differential Algorithm', 'definition' => 'A step-by-step clinical decision tree used to systematically exclude competing diagnoses based on symptoms, duration, and course.'],
            ['term' => 'Psychosis-to-Mood Ratio', 'definition' => 'The quantitative proportion of total illness duration during which mood symptoms are present relative to active and residual psychotic symptoms.'],
            ['term' => 'Transient Stress-Related Paranoia', 'definition' => 'Fleeting paranoid cognitions occurring in response to intense interpersonal abandonment fears, classically seen in Borderline Personality Disorder.'],
            ['term' => 'Ego-Syntonic', 'definition' => 'Psychological states, ideas, or behaviors that are experienced as acceptable, natural, and consistent with the fundamental self-concept.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Board-Exam Synthesis',
        'slug' => 'chapter-mastery-board-exam-synthesis-schizophrenia',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of psychotic spectrum disorders, diagnostic criteria, duration parameters, and high-yield licensure exam pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit delivers an advanced synthesis of Schizophrenia Spectrum and Other Psychotic Disorders under DSM-5 criteria, consolidating core diagnostic formulations, duration thresholds, and high-yield board pearls for professional licensure examinations. Psychotic spectrum disorders represent some of the most rigorously tested topics on psychiatric board examinations, demanding rapid and precise identification of temporal milestones, diagnostic exclusions, and course specifiers.\n\nMastery requires commanding the clinical distinctions separating Brief Psychotic Disorder, Schizophreniform Disorder, Schizophrenia, and Schizoaffective Disorder. Furthermore, candidates must navigate the updated DSM-5 structural revisions, including the elimination of the five classical schizophrenia subtypes, the removal of the Schneiderian single-symptom exception, and the establishment of Catatonia as a transdiagnostic specifier.\n\nBy synthesizing clinical vignettes, diagnostic hierarchies, and pharmacological principles, this unit ensures complete readiness for complex board exam questions and real-world clinical consultations.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Mastery Summary of Diagnostic Parameters:\n\n1. Brief Psychotic Disorder: >= 1 positive symptom (delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, catatonia); duration >= 1 day and < 1 month; full return to premorbid functioning.\n\n2. Schizophreniform Disorder: >= 2 Criterion A symptoms (at least one being delusions, hallucinations, or disorganized speech); duration >= 1 month and < 6 months; functional decline NOT required.\n\n3. Schizophrenia: Continuous signs of disturbance for >= 6 months, including >= 1 month of active Criterion A symptoms; documented social/occupational decline required; classic subtypes eliminated.\n\n4. Schizoaffective Disorder: Major mood episode concurrent with Criterion A; delusions/hallucinations for >= 2 weeks in absence of mood symptoms; mood symptoms present for majority of total illness duration.\n\n5. Delusional Disorder: >= 1 delusion for >= 1 month; functioning not markedly impaired; behavior not bizarre outside delusional scope; 7 clinical subtypes.\n\n6. Catatonia: >= 3 of 12 psychomotor features; transdiagnostic specifier.",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: 'With marked stressor(s)' in Brief Psychotic Disorder; 'With good prognostic features' in Schizophreniform Disorder; Bipolar vs. Depressive types in Schizoaffective Disorder; 7 thematic subtypes in Delusional Disorder; and transdiagnostic Catatonia specifier.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key Board Differential Pearls:\n• Psychosis for 3 weeks -> Brief Psychotic Disorder.\n• Psychosis for 3 months -> Schizophreniform Disorder.\n• Psychosis for 7 months -> Schizophrenia.\n• Hallucinations persist 3 weeks after depression resolves -> Schizoaffective Disorder.\n• Hallucinations resolve immediately when depression resolves -> Major Depressive Disorder with Psychotic Features.\n• Delusions of infidelity with normal occupational performance -> Delusional Disorder, Jealous type.\n• Stupor, catalepsy, and waxy flexibility in bipolar mania -> Bipolar I Disorder, with Catatonia.",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review vignette: A 23-year-old patient presents with three months of auditory hallucinations, persecutory delusions, and flat affect. The patient quit their job, but family notes that six weeks ago, the patient experienced a two-week period of depressed mood and insomnia.\n\nBoard Exam Question Formulation: Does the patient have Schizoaffective Disorder or Schizophreniform Disorder? The candidate evaluates the longitudinal proportion: the depressive symptoms lasted only two weeks out of three months (a minor fraction, not the majority). Therefore, the correct diagnosis is Schizophreniform Disorder (Provisional), successfully applying DSM-5 longitudinal criteria.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: Brief Psychotic (<1 mo) vs. Schizophreniform (1-6 mos) vs. Schizophrenia (>=6 mos). The 2-week rule (psychosis without mood) establishes Schizoaffective Disorder. Depressed mood (A1) is mandatory for depressive episodes in Schizoaffective disorder. Catatonia requires 3 of 12 signs and is most common in mood disorders. DSM-5 eliminated schizophrenia subtypes and Schneiderian single-symptom rules.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Mastery Matrix of Psychotic Disorders', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive integration of duration parameters, symptom counts, and functional criteria across all psychotic entities.'],
            ['title' => 'The 2-Week Litmus Test', 'icon' => '🧪', 'definition' => 'Delusions/hallucinations for >= 2 weeks without mood disturbance proves independent psychotic disorder.'],
            ['title' => 'DSM-5 Structural Modernization', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Commanding the removal of schizophrenia subtypes, Schneiderian exceptions, and addition of dimensional severity ratings.'],
            ['title' => 'Emergency Catatonia Recognition', 'icon' => '🚨', 'definition' => 'Identifying the 12 psychomotor signs and initiating rapid benzodiazepine or ECT protocols for malignant presentations.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Psychotic Spectrum', 'definition' => 'The continuum of psychiatric conditions defined by impairments in reality testing, including delusions, hallucinations, and disorganized thinking.'],
            ['term' => 'Schneiderian Criteria', 'definition' => 'Historical first-rank symptoms of schizophrenia eliminated as single-symptom diagnostic criteria in DSM-5.'],
            ['term' => 'Longitudinal Course Specifier', 'definition' => 'Standardized DSM-5 descriptors recording the chronological pattern of acute episodes and remissions over time.'],
            ['term' => 'Ativan Challenge Test', 'definition' => 'The administration of 1-2 mg intravenous lorazepam to rapidly verify and temporarily alleviate catatonic motor signs.']
        ]
    ]
];
